use Workspaces from @data
use WordFlowerDrawerNavigator, WordFlowerNavigator, WordFlowerTabsNavigator from @nav
use InstantDB from @tao/data/providers/instantdb
use Local from @tao/data/providers/local
use Spinner, TextMultiline from @tao/ui
use SavedToast, WordFlowerShell, WorkspaceRow from @ui

// Tranche status: absorbed
//
// PHRASES. A `phrase` is named copy that returns text: one sentence with typed holes, or plural
// forms (`one`, `other`, and the other CLDR categories, `other` required) chosen by its one number
// parameter. The outline's count reads "1 paragraph" and "0 paragraphs". Extracting copy for
// translation and `words` blocks are Post-MVP.
//
// READ NET. Every app has a read net for the exceptional read cases a guard site does not name:
// loading, missing, unauthorized, and error. The runtime supplies it; a file-level `guard default`
// restyles it case by case, as WordFlower does for loading and error. A bare `guard Subject` sends
// every exceptional case to the net, and a site names only the cases it treats specially.
//
// SEARCH. A field marked `(search)` is part of its entity's search corpus, and a query's
// `search <term>` keeps the rows whose search fields match the term with the matcher keyboard
// narrowing uses: locale-aware word prefixes, in order. A blank term keeps every row; the query's
// order and limit apply afterwards. Grouping (`group by`) is Post-MVP.
//
// WRITE RULES. `required "<sentence>"` states completeness: it never blocks a write, and it derives
// `Incomplete` and `Problems` on a row and on every projection that selects the field. A form for a
// new row edits a projection (`type WorkspaceInput is Workspace { Name }`), `create Workspace with
// Input` writes it, and the stdlib `Problems(Input.Problems)` lists what is still missing, in the
// order the entity states its rules and nothing once the input is complete. `validate` and `refuse
// when` are Post-MVP.
//
// CHECK. `check <boolean>` is an action's early exit: when the expression is false, the rest of that
// action is skipped. Nothing fails, nothing is reported, and a caller that reached it through `do`
// carries on. It is a statement in actions only; `guard` stays the view-side construct and tests a
// data object's availability, never a boolean. A notice that explains the stop is an ordinary `if`
// before the `check`, as `AddWorkspace` shows.
//
// REACTIVE EDITING. Input projections and explicit copies produce ordinary local values.
// Writable view/action parameters share storage; copy parameters detach explicitly. Native
// mutable inputs write through generated Tao actions. On granular sync providers, document write
// outcomes retain pending and failed submissions and retry their recorded payloads across
// relaunch. Snapshot-provider recovery and server-enforced validation are deferred to
// Docs/Roadmap/Reactive editing follow-up.md.
//
// FREEHAND UI SKETCHING L1. Scenario entries are journey prefixes: ordered interaction steps follow
// the effective `run` or `render` subject, replay when the Studio cell mounts, and leave it
// interactive in the reached state. `press down`, `press up`, and `hover` accept the same text,
// label, placeholder, and #tag selectors as `press`; `focus` is tag-only. Plain `press` remains a
// complete activation; `enter`, `submit`, and tagged-row `select` retain their test spellings. A
// required action argument may be omitted only from a scenario render, where
// Studio supplies a per-cell logging stand-in. A fixture is optional and means an empty store; it is
// required only when `prepare` or a render argument references a fixture handle.
//
// `Placeholder(Label)` is the stdlib's explicit unfinished-content leaf: labelled and hatched in
// development, empty at its declared size in release, and warned about by `tao check`. `Spacer()` is
// the semantic flexible-space leaf, with implicit `claim 1` and an overridable `[claim N]`.
//
// PROJECT RELEASE METADATA. This tranche gives local release tooling the source-owned version and
// default runnable app it needs without making either value part of runtime behavior.
//
// This tranche decides:
// - `version` is the version currently under development, written as numeric SemVer core only:
//   exactly `major.minor.patch`, with no leading zeroes, prerelease, or build suffix
// - `DefaultApp` names one app declaration from this project. Command-line `--app` selection still
//   overrides it, and projects may omit it when interactive selection is preferable
//
// STUDIO SCENARIOS. This tranche implements the decided fixture and app-running scenario subset
// shared by Tao tests, review tooling, and Studio. `fixture` owns reusable data/identity setup;
// `scenarios Subject "group"` collects string-identified entries under shared defaults; each entry
// may override any clause. Studio adds no `example` declaration or sidecar authority.
//
// The approved focused subject is `render ViewName(Parameter: FixtureHandle)`. Its named arguments
// bind the view's declared parameters, and every scenario has exactly one effective app or view subject.
// Runtime lowering begins with deterministic fixture data plus viewport, appearance, locale,
// direction, and the explicitly spelled online/offline network pins. The clock value spelling remains
// deferred rather than guessed. Scheme resolves reactively through the runtime and mounted design.
//
// SURFACES AND DECLARATIONS. This tranche gives WordFlower the UI primitives, adaptive layout,
// declaration model, and the absorbed design catalog plus Studio Slice 6 structured subset used here.
//
// The contract is now a DIRECTORY. Every mapped source and test file here is part of it:
//
//   WordFlower       this entry file — project, apps, navigation, datasources, variants, shell
//   Design           the design catalog and structured forcing subset — implemented last, see that file
//   @ui/             the workspace, document, and focus-session areas
//   @nav/            the navigation values and the deterministic harness navigator
//   @data/           the data catalog
//   *.test           the behavior journeys, each beside what it exercises
//
// Declarations carry the narrowest visibility that works. A file-private declaration carries no
// marker; `folder` reaches the rest of this directory with no `use` line, which is why the sibling
// import lists are gone. Entities, apps, and case sets still resolve through their own paths, so
// those keep an explicit `use`. `ui` and `nav` are separate folders, so what they publish across a
// boundary is `workspace` and is imported by path.
//
// Each tranche's scratch contracts graduate before its boundary: the Prelude and `@tao/text` to
// `packages/stdlib/@tao` and
// `packages/stdlib/@tao/text`, and this tranche's `duration` primitive and `@tao/time` to the
// Prelude and `packages/stdlib/@tao/time`. Their `@tao-next/` copies are intentionally absent at
// this absorbed boundary.
//
// The absorption gates must compare `1 - Current` and `2 - Next` as whole directories: the same
// file set, with every file byte-identical to its counterpart when the tranche closes. Implementing
// directory gating was part of this tranche. The repeated sidecar markers were bootstrap input and
// were removed when the directory contract gained one status.
//
// This tranche decides:
// - Stdlib surfaces: `ScrollView`, `Image`, `Checkbox`, `Spinner`, and `Progress`. Selectable
//   collection rendering stays language-owned: `loop Plural / Singular { ... }` scopes its template
//   block and optional `on select` to the row, so no stdlib `List` or function-typed row slot is
//   introduced
// - Text stdlib: `@tao/text` exports the injected functions `CountWords(Value is text)` and
//   `Join(Values is list of text, Separator is text)` with the exact behavior specified by
//   `packages/stdlib/tao/text/Text.tao`
// - Adaptive layout: `Panes()` lays its children side by side when, after gaps, each child can
//   receive at least 320 logical pixels; otherwise it stacks them in source order. In horizontal
//   mode, `claim` distributes remaining width proportionally. `width max <n>` bounds a readable
//   column. This adaptation is fixed stdlib container behavior, not language breakpoint syntax
// - `async { }` runs its block without delaying the statements after it; failures surface as
//   provider error state
// - Primitive value heads make the common product declaration explicit: `nav N = StackNav { ... }`,
//   `datasource S = Local { ... }`, and `app X = Y with { ... }` declare values, while the rarer
//   reusable type form is `type T is nav|datasource|app with { ... }`. A direct `nav N { ... }`
//   instantiates primitive `nav`; it fills only members `nav` already declares. It never declares a
//   new nav type or adds members, and it is usable only when those fills plus primitive defaults make
//   the value complete. `let` remains the universal auto-typed immutable binding for local,
//   supporting, computed, and copied values; a primitive head constrains the value family without
//   widening away the more precise inferred type
// - `with` is THE core refinement operation underlying type derivation, type instantiation, and
//   value derivation. It may be omitted only for construction from a base resolved as a type:
//   `Type { <fills> }` is sugar for `Type with { <fills> }`. Derivation from an existing value must
//   keep it visible as `Value with { <delta> }`; `Value { ... }` is not legal. If type and value
//   namespaces both contain a name, bare `Name { ... }` resolves the type namespace and constructs
//   a value. In a value expression, `Name with { ... }` resolves the value namespace first and the
//   type namespace only when no value exists; after `type T is`, the base resolves only the type
//   namespace. The delta always narrows or fills and never reopens a filled member. A value may fill
//   or override declared data/configuration properties but may never introduce a member
//   No `required` keyword is introduced: a supplied property without a default remains required
// - App values use ordinary declaration placement and visibility, including across project and
//   package modules. `run`, imports, and strict app identity accept any complete app value uniformly,
//   whether a primitive head or an inferred `let` declared it; no AST-kind union leaks into semantics
// - A render site composes named clause bundles and direct clauses in one list, and the last
//   specification of a given clause wins: `[screen, claim 2, width max 720]` reads left to right.
//   After same-clause replacement, validation rejects a resolved clause set whose remaining clauses
//   are semantically incompatible; source order never makes incompatible clause categories valid
// - [Superseded by UNIFIED VIEW below.] This tranche introduced the kind hierarchy — `visual` and
//   `presentable` roles, `ui`/`nav` refining `presentable`, `view`/`layout`/`frame` refining
//   `visual`, content acceptance belonging to the kind — that the unified view tranche later
//   collapsed into the one `view` primitive. What survives unchanged: a layout clause is ambient on
//   every view occurrence, so nothing declares it and no user name collides with it, and
//   `packages/stdlib/@tao/Prelude.tao` pins the primitives
// - A view declares an optional named content slot with `@name = empty`. The name is in the view's
//   slot namespace, `empty` contributes no rendered node, and a caller fills it at most once with
//   `@name <view>`. A filled slot is opaque visual content placed exactly where `@name` occurs in
//   the declaring render body; `Card` and its `@actions` call site exercise the complete contract
// - An injection has no compiler-prefixed names. `TR`, `RN`, and `process` are always in scope in
//   injected TypeScript; the inject list explicitly names every additional Tao-provided binding.
//   Each entry is `<Name> <value>` over any expression, and a bare name binds a property under its
//   own name:
//   `render inject Total, Suffix " words", Content @@content, Layout @@layout, Tag @@tag`.
//   This retires the implicit `_ViewProps` bag and is implemented across Tao, stdlib included. The
//   ambient channels are exactly `@@content`, `@@layout` — which carries the parent
//   direction the occurrence lays out against — and `@@tag`; navigation, response, app, and caller
//   inheritance stay private and never reach a hand-written implementation
// - The value of `render inject` is the view's root element and Tao wraps nothing around it, which
//   is already the emitted behavior. Applying `Layout` and `Tag`, and rendering `Content` exactly
//   once, are the implementation's responsibility and are not checked
// - Every `view`, `action`, and `function` declaration has a parenthesized parameter list,
//   including `()` when empty, mirroring its call sites: `view WordCountLabel(Value text)`,
//   `action SaveDocument()`, `view Settings()`. Omitting the list is invalid. A `function` has a
//   block body and hands its result back with `return`, so early returns are available;
//   `returns <type>` is optional and inferred when omitted
// - `let X is Type = value` ascribes a binding: `is` narrows, `=` binds. It keeps aligned `let`
//   columns and preserves `Name is Type` order rather than inverting to type-first
// - List types always name their element type as `list of T`; bare `list` is invalid in type
//   declarations and parameter ascriptions
// - `type Name is one of Cases` declares a nominal type in the ordinary type system, and each case is a
//   value of that type. One-case enums are valid; `ConfirmResult` deliberately uses `none` for
//   dismissal rather than declaring a second case
// - `optional <Name> <type>` declares an item field that may be absent
// - `expect checkbox <selector> checked|unchecked` joins the test vocabulary, since a two-state
//   control cannot be asserted through visible text
// - Minimal design: flat tokens plus named clause bundles applied at render sites, and an app
//   `Design` property. The lexer replaces `TAG` with one shared terminal,
//   `TagOrHexColor: /#[A-Za-z0-9_]+/`. AST context distinguishes tag and design-token positions.
//   The validator requires tags to match `#[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*` and design color values to match
//   CSS hexadecimal `#RGB`, `#RGBA`, `#RRGGBB`, or `#RRGGBBAA`, case-insensitively
//
// FOCUSED WRITING. This tranche gives WordFlower a focused writing session — pick a length, write
// until the clock runs out, pause and resume, or stop early — and with it the first capability cut
// from the Current ↔ MVP gap: unit values (Decisions §2) and the ticking clock (§9), reached
// through the expression-position TypeScript boundary (§15) and proved under a controllable test
// clock (§16).
//
// Earlier tranches' decisions are recorded in `Docs/Spec/` and `Docs/Roadmap/Tao Revolution/Decisions.md`;
// this header lists only what this tranche decides:
// - Unit values (§2): `.unit` on a number constructs a typed value and `.unit` on a typed value
//   reads it back as a number in that unit, so `10.min`, `1.s`, and `220.ms` construct durations
//   and `Wait.s` reads one. A value belongs to one family with a canonical base and fixed ratios.
//   The mechanism is generic; `duration` — `ms`, `s`, `min`, `h`, `d`, `wk`, with §2's long singular
//   and plural aliases — is the only family this tranche registers. Equality normalizes
//   (`60.s == 1.min`); a cross-family read is a compile error; arithmetic is dimensional: duration
//   ± duration → duration, duration × number → duration, duration ÷ duration → number, and anything
//   else is a compile error, except that a unit value compares against the bare literal `0`
// - Calendar arithmetic (§2): `now` is an ordinary expression anywhere, not only a `time` default;
//   `time − time → duration` and `time + duration → time`
// - `duration` is a primitive type in the Prelude, used wherever a type goes: a parameter
//   `Length duration`, a `returns duration`, a `state` or `let` holding one
// - `.Clock` reads a duration as clock text: whole seconds, `m:ss` under an hour and `h:mm:ss` from
//   an hour up, and `0:00` for zero or negative
// - `@tao/time` (§9): `use Interval from @tao/time` imports its declarations by name, like every
//   other package, and `Interval(Every)` constructs a reactive ticker that starts when the view
//   holding it mounts and stops when that view unmounts. `Tick.Value` is the time as of the latest
//   tick, `Tick.Running` whether it ticks, and `do Tick.Stop()` and `do Tick.Start()` control it.
//   No clock grammar enters the language. This amends Decisions §9 and §11, which sketched a
//   lowercase service import (`use time from @tao/time`, `time.Interval(1.s)`): a package binding
//   would need a second `use` form and a package-as-namespace value, and the segment names of the
//   packages that already exist — `ui`, `nav`, `data` — were Tao keywords when this was decided
//   (`nav` and `data` still are; UNIFIED VIEW below retires `ui`), so the shape does not
//   generalize to the packages it would first apply to
// - A `let` over a ticker is a live derivation: `let Left = EndsAt - Tick.Value` recomputes on
//   every tick, and every display that reads it re-renders
// - `<expression> from <path>` (§15) in expression position, binding loosest: the expression is a
//   name or a call to one, that head name is the sidecar's named export, and any arguments are
//   ordinary Tao expressions. An arbitrary expression to the left would leave every name in it
//   ambiguous between Tao scope and module scope; §15's own examples all name the export as the
//   head. This is how `@tao/time` binds its runtime. Value-position `inject <type> … ```ts` retires
//   with it — `@tao/text` and `BuildStamp` migrate — while `render inject` stays as the view
//   authoring mode
// - The compact two-outcome `when <boolean> <Yes> / not <No>` (§8) is the value form a display of
//   `Left` needs: `when Left > 0 Left.Clock / not "Time's up"`
// - A toast's `Duration` is a duration: `present … as toast (Key: "…", Duration: 3.s)`
// - Tests (§16): every test starts from a fresh, fixed clock, and `advance <duration>` moves it,
//   firing every interval tick that falls due, in order, so a ticking journey is deterministic.
//   Toast expiry is thereby testable and is proved in the documents journey
//
// UNIFIED VIEW. This tranche collapses the renderable declaration kinds into the single `view`
// kind, implementing the unified view amendment to `Docs/Roadmap/Tao Revolution/Decisions.md`
// (§2, §8, §9, §10, §13).
//
// This tranche decides:
// - One renderable declaration kind: `view`. The keywords `ui`, `frame`, `layout`, and `dialogue`
//   retire with nothing replacing them — no marker, no modifier, no new kind. A view declares a
//   parameterized body or passes through with `view X = pkg.Member`, and may declare `responds T`;
//   the clause survives the retired `dialogue` kind
// - Presentation is a property of the call site, never the declaration. A view composes inline in
//   a render tree, or is presented (`present X(…) as overlay | sheet | toast`, `present X(…) in
//   <nav>`, `ask X(…)`), with the mode chosen where the presenting happens. Presenting, dismissing,
//   replacing, and selection activation are legal in any view body, which is why `WorkspaceRow`
//   and `DraftRow` now open their own subjects instead of receiving an `Open action()`
// - Capabilities are inferred from the declaration, never declared. A view accepts caller content
//   iff its body places `@@content` — in its render tree, or by naming the `@@content` channel in
//   its `render inject` list; it offers named render slots iff its body declares them with
//   `@name = empty`; it answers `ask` iff it declares `responds T`, and `respond` is legal only in
//   a view that does
// - `@@content` placement is at most once, and a placement may be conditional: while its branch is
//   off, the caller's content does not mount. Passing child content to a view that places no
//   `@@content` remains the leaf-content error, now inferred from the body rather than the kind
// - One body grammar. State, entity queries, actions, aliases, tags, slots, and render are legal
//   in any view body; the frame/layout statelessness ladder is deleted. `Collapsible` is the
//   stateful content-accepting wrapper the ladder forbade
// - The primitive types `visual`, `presentable`, `ui`, `frame`, and `layout` collapse into the
//   single primitive `view`; `nav` now refines `view`, and `datasource` and `app` are untouched.
//   Everything that accepted a presentable — `Initial`, `Content`, `present`, `replace` — accepts
//   a view, and every view is a value, so the stdlib's `Presentable` union type retires
// - The frame-only occurrence layout default (`hug`/`rigid`) retires with the kind: a view
//   occurrence takes only the clauses written at its call site, the same for every view
// - Restorability is usage-derived. No serialization boundary ships in this tranche, so nothing
//   here validates it yet; the decision that matters now is its direction — the requirement and
//   its diagnostic belong to the usage site, never the declaration — which is why an ephemeral
//   presentation may take a non-serializable parameter: `SavedToast` receives the revert `action`
//   for the very save it announces
// - [Superseded by HOST-READ VIEW SLOTS AND NATIVE NAV KIT below.] This tranche originally deferred
//   command focus scoping and restoration together. The host-read tranche now lands the minimal
//   focused command vertical forced by `Toolbar`; restoration policy remains a later tranche
//
// HOST-READ VIEW SLOTS AND NATIVE NAV KIT. This tranche gives every view a typed, reactive way to
// describe itself to the host that directly presents it, then makes native navigation the standard
// kit while retaining an explicit deterministic basic implementation.
//
// This tranche decides:
// - `Title` and `Toolbar` are optional supplied slots on primitive `view`, declared by the Prelude
//   rather than compiler-owned vocabulary. `nav is view` inherits them; stdlib hosts name only the
//   slots in their fixed read-set. A view fills
//   one with an ordinary capitalized body member (`Title Workspace.Name`, `Toolbar { Save }`); no
//   declaration modifier or content-side metadata keyword is introduced
// - Slot values are live expressions over that presented occurrence's parameters, reads, and state.
//   The host updates its chrome when a value changes. It reads only the directly presented view:
//   descendants never bubble values, wrappers fill their own slots, and a presenter cannot override
//   a fill at a call site
// - Every `StackNav` entry, both `Initial` and later pushes, reads `Title` and `Toolbar` and requires
//   `Title`. `present … as window` has that same contract, including its full-screen fallback.
//   `Toolbar` remains optional. `SlotNav`, `SelectionNav`, `SplitNav`, and sheet, root, menu, and toast
//   presentations read neither slot. Missing required fills are diagnosed at the placement, because
//   the view itself remains valid in a host that does not require them
// - [Superseded by the interaction system tranche. `Title`, `Description`, and `Summary` moved off
//   `primitive action` onto `primitive command`; a command became a standalone configured value with
//   its own slots, and `intent` retired as a word. See Decisions §8.] An `action` or `transaction` is
//   an intent when it supplies `Title`; no `intent` keyword exists.
//   A `command` is a focused reference to an intent, never a second behavior body. It may supply
//   `Label`, `Icon`, `Key`, and boolean `Enabled`; each takes an ordinary reactive expression and
//   `Label` defaults to the intent's `Title`. A command
//   nested in a view closes over that occurrence and remains active while its nearest presented
//   occurrence holds focus. This tranche implements the action metadata, bound command, `Icon`,
//   reactive `Enabled`, label default, and `Toolbar` listing that WordFlower and HNReader force; broader
//   menus, rails, palettes, assistants, and restoration remain later work
// - Native toolbars use trailing platform controls and basic/web toolbars use styled Tao controls.
//   Commands retain source order. The current mobile/basic policy keeps the first two controls
//   direct and moves the trailing suffix into a `More` affordance in the same order; a future wider
//   host may expose more direct controls without reordering them. It never clips, drops, or silently
//   disables commands
// - `@tao/nav` mirrors `@tao/ui`: `native/` owns platform navigation and chrome, `basic/` owns the
//   portable clause-honoring rendering, and the package root publishes native by default. Native and
//   basic implementations of a family expose the same configuration, behavior, and host read-set
// - A configurable type may transparently publish another package member with
//   `public type StackNav = native.StackNav`. This is a pass-through, not nominal derivation: the
//   alias preserves the target declaration's identity, inferred interface, primitive family,
//   configuration contract, host read-set, and runtime implementation
// - Product code keeps bare `@tao/nav` imports. The deterministic navigation harness imports
//   `@tao/nav/basic` explicitly. Under behavior tests or on a target without the native host, a native
//   kind uses the same synchronous semantic reducer and basic host surface; focused native adapter
//   tests exercise platform options, transitions, and callbacks separately
// - Journeys observe host-owned chrome rather than matching duplicate body text:
//   `expect navigation title "WordFlower"` asserts the directly presented title,
//   `expect toolbar command "Save workspace" enabled|disabled` asserts a visible command state, and
//   `press toolbar command "Save workspace"` invokes that command through the host surface
//
// THE INTERACTION SYSTEM, PART ONE: SCENES, THE FRAME, AND COMMANDS. This tranche lands the two
// independent halves the interaction system rests on (`Docs/Roadmap/Keyboard driven apps/
// Design - Keyboard driven apps.md`, KEY-D1-D14; `Plan - Keyboard driven apps.md`, T1 and T2).
// They are one tranche because they migrate the same screens: T1 turns presented views into scenes
// and T2 rewrites their commands, so each WordFlower file moves once.
//
// The forcing features are WordFlower's persistent focus bar and its workspace save command.
//
// This tranche decides:
// - `scene is view`: the one home for host-facing chrome. `Title`, `Toolbar`, and `Header` are
//   supplied slots on `scene` alone, so a `view` that is only ever composed cannot declare chrome
//   that nothing reads. A scene is presented, never composed inline, diagnosed at the render site.
//   A plain view may still be pushed or presented anywhere and shows Back-only header chrome; a
//   scene pushed on a StackNav or presented `as window` must fill `Title`; sheets read neither
//   `Title` nor `Toolbar`.
//   `nav is scene`
// - `Header` is the explicit opt out of header chrome: `Header false` on a scene suppresses the
//   header bar entirely, for a screen that owns its full surface. Back still works through the
//   reducer, the gesture, and the hardware key -- only the bar is gone. A scene with a literal
//   `Header false` may not fill `Title` or `Toolbar`, because that chrome would be dead, and it is
//   exempt from the pushed-scene `Title` requirement
// - [Superseded by the revised shell decision below.] The frame is a fifth nav kind: `FrameNav`,
//   fixed chrome edges around a navigated center.
//   Its slots are `@top`, `@bottom`, `@left`, `@right`, and `@center`, holding views or navs.
//   `left` and `right` are the language's existing layout terms and reverse with the locale exactly
//   as they do in a layout clause. Horizontal bars win the corners: `@top` and `@bottom` span the
//   full width and `@left`/`@right` occupy the space between them. Each slot takes a `Label`, a
//   `Content`, and an optional `Size` -- one dimension, because the edge already fixes the axis, so
//   the meaningless one is unwritable. Absent `Size` means content-derived
// - The empty-slot rule is semantic, never measured: a slot whose `Content` evaluates to `none`
//   reserves no space. That makes the focus bar's presence a reactive property of the data rather
//   than a layout accident. Bars never take Back; the frame routes Back to `@center`. SplitNav
//   keeps its own job -- navigational peers that progress and fold -- and a frame edge is fixed
//   chrome
// - `local only` is an entity-level storage fact, a third trailing group entry beside `index` and
//   `order by`: the entity lives in the device store whatever datasource the app configures, so a
//   synced variant never syncs it. It is the narrow form of per-entity datasource scoping
// - [Superseded by Interaction Cleanup below.] A `query` may be declared at module level: an
//   app-lifetime reactive read of the store, and an
//   ordinary value. A nav is configuration, so there is no view between the shell frame and the
//   store; without this the frame could not ask whether a focus session is running
// - `command` is a configured value, in the `app`/`nav`/`design` family, and the only discoverable
//   verb. Its parameters are slots, its metadata members are `Title` (static), `Description`,
//   `Summary`, `Label` (reactive, defaulting to `Title`), `Icon`, `Key`, and `Enabled`, and its
//   behavior is exactly one `do` of a named or inline action. It is declared at module level or in
//   a view body, the same rule as `action`, and a view-body command closes over that view
// - `action` is private: it loses `Title`, `Description`, and `Summary`, and is never surfaced.
//   "An action is an intent" is retired, and so is `command X = Action(args) with { ... }`
// - Inside a command block, juxtaposition declares a slot and a name with a value fills a member --
//   the same namespace rule Decisions section 2 already uses. `Document` alone is a slot taking its
//   same-named type; `Track Document` is a named slot; `Title "Delete"` is a fill. A name in both
//   the type and value namespaces asks for the explicit `Name: value` form
// - `Value with { ... }` fills a command's slots and may override `Label`, `Icon`, `Key`, and
//   `Enabled`, never `Title`; `do <command> with { ... }` invokes one, and `do` on an unfilled slot
//   is a diagnostic
// - `shortcut` is the value type of `Key`. `key` was taken -- it is the slot-key type in
//   `SelectionNav { Initial key }`. `primary + "n"` is a shortcut, and a bare string in `Key`
//   position is a shortcut literal. Only the platform-abstract `primary` modifier exists; naming a
//   platform's own modifier is a diagnostic
// - `Toolbar` is `list of command` and moves to `scene` with `Title`. Every module publishes a
//   catalog of its commands, which is what makes "every command whose slots this target can fill"
//   answerable at all -- the verb universe the later tranches read
//
// THE INTERACTION SYSTEM, PART ONE, REVISED: THE SHELL IS A VIEW, AND A COMMAND HAS PARAMETERS. Two
// decisions the first part implemented are reversed after review (`Docs/Roadmap/Keyboard driven
// apps/Open - Shell composition and member syntax.md`). Both make the language smaller.
//
// This tranche decides:
// - A render site may name a nav. `nav is scene is view`, and the grammar now agrees: everything
//   that renders a view renders a nav, so a shell is ordinary layout -- a `Col` holding the
//   navigator and a bar -- and `FrameNav` retires with nothing replacing it. A rendered nav still
//   owns its occurrences, Back, restoration, and chrome; only where it sits in the tree changes
// - Three invariants keep a rendered nav honest, all diagnosed at the render site: a nav renders at
//   most once, never inside a loop, and never inside a conditional branch -- its history lives on its
//   mount, so a branch that unmounts it would silently drop where the person was
// - The app root is a view with arguments: `view WordFlowerShell(WordFlowerNavigator)`. A view-typed
//   or nav-typed parameter renders like any other view, which is how one shell serves every preview
//   variant without three copies of the bar
// - The `@name` sigil belongs to named render slots -- content a caller passes into a view that lays
//   it out and owns the interaction. A frame's edges were never that, which is the other reason the
//   frame goes
// - A command declares its slots in a parameter list, exactly as an action does:
//   `command Finish(Document) { ... }`. The block then holds only member fills and one `do`, so
//   juxtaposition means one thing inside it -- bind -- and the two ambiguities the first part had
//   to legislate around dissolve: `command Like(Track Song)` renames a typed slot, and
//   `Enabled CanSave` is a value again with no `:` escape hatch
// - `do Finish(Document)` invokes a command, mirroring `do SaveWorkspace()`; `Finish with { Document }`
//   derives a command value with a slot filled, for surfaces and menus. `do X with { }` retires as
//   redundant
//
// THE INTERACTION SYSTEM, PART TWO: THE OUTLINE AND `(title)`. The runtime now keeps a derived,
// read-only model of the running app -- regions, collections, their items, action controls, input
// controls -- each with identity, a label, and provenance, and accessibility is its first reader
// (`Design - Keyboard driven apps.md`, KEY-D2, D4, D5, D6; `Plan`, T3).
//
// Almost nothing here is declared. The one construct is a trait; everything else is derived from
// the ordinary structure of the app, which is the whole point.
//
// This tranche decides:
// - `(title)` is a data-field trait: the one field that names an entity to a person, for the
//   palette, the OS, and the fallback row label. At most one per entity, and only on `text`
// - An item's label is derived, one computation shared with accessibility: the first
//   unconditional `Text` in the row whose value is a member path on the row -- preferring the
//   `(title)` field when the row renders it -- then the `(title)` field read at runtime, then the
//   entity's name and handle. A function-wrapped path is opaque and passes to the next candidate.
//   A selectable row carries that label to the platform; a journey may `expect label` it
// - Node kinds are derived from wiring, never marked: a `loop` is a collection and each row an
//   item; a render binding `Press` or `Submit`, or a row with `on select`, is an action control;
//   a render binding `Value` with `Change` is an input control. Regions are structural: selection
//   items, split panes, and presented occurrences. The non-nav content of a view that renders a
//   nav is not yet a region; the attention tranche below decides and adds that region
// - Existence is always registered; the label is always on, because accessibility needs it
//   synchronously; everything else evaluates only while a consumer is attached
// - Provenance rides two tiers -- entity and handle per item, loop and entity per collection --
//   and is never a test selector
//
// INTERACTION CLEANUP. The shell's move into an ordinary scene left three general mechanisms without
// a forcing feature (`Design - Keyboard driven apps.md`; `Plan`, T3½).
//
// This tranche decides:
// - A module-level `query` is not a declaration in the language today. The parser recognizes that
//   shape only so the validator can explain that it is not supported, could be added, and has no
//   strict technical obstacle; it creates no export, declaration, or runtime binding. Each scene or
//   view that needs the value owns an ordinary local query instead, as the three `CurrentSession`
//   reads below demonstrate
// - A `when` used as a configuration value compiles directly as its value expression. The generic
//   `TR.Deferred` wrapper retires; `TR.Interaction.Deferred` is a separate interaction-runtime
//   mechanism and remains
// - A direct keyed entry in a configured-value patch replaces the whole item and must be complete.
//   Special deep merging of partial keyed frame slots retires with the frame
// - Absence unification in `commonType`, union-to-union assignability, and property-position `with`
//   remain as general improvements
//
// THE INTERACTION SYSTEM, PART THREE: ATTENTION, KEYBOARD DISPATCH, NARROWING, AND SURFACING.
// This tranche makes the read-only outline operable without giving any consumer a mutation path
// through it (`Design - Keyboard driven apps.md`, KEY-D8-D13; `Plan`, T4).
//
// This tranche decides:
// - One runtime-owned attention reducer holds region focus, one remembered target per region,
//   narrowing text and candidates, engagement, and the modal focus stack. Navigating, narrowing,
//   engaged, verb-pending, overview, and hints are derived modes, never stored Tao state. Attention
//   is reset between launches and tests and is neither settable nor restored
// - The public outline remains snapshots only. Mounted nodes additionally give the reducer a
//   private live channel for activation, native focus/engagement, geometry, render order, and the
//   actual row value needed to bind an entity command. Provenance remains read-only metadata and is
//   never used as a selector or mutation handle. Generated descriptors stay in generated modules
//   and are passed into handwritten `TR.Interaction`; nothing generated attaches to `TR`
// - Regions are presented occurrences, selection items, split panes, and -- settled here -- the
//   non-nav sibling subtree of a view that renders a nav. The compiler emits one descriptor for
//   that subtree and its roots coalesce under one ref-counted runtime registration; no wrapper or
//   layout node is added. This is required because WordFlower's focus-bar controls otherwise have
//   no region, contradicting modality-neutral focus, narrowing, overview, and re-aim. The sibling
//   region is named by its first derived label (`Focus session` here); the rendered nav keeps its
//   own region
// - Targeting is eager and free: moving or narrowing may commit a target, and one remaining
//   candidate auto-targets, but only Enter/activation or a verb performs behavior. Candidates are
//   mounted items and controls in the focused region. Narrowing is case-insensitive, locale-aware
//   word-prefix subsequence matching over full rendered text; spaces advance to later words.
//   Narrowing does not unmount or currently restyle nonmatches
// - Arrows move region/target attention; Enter activates or engages; Escape clears narrowing,
//   disengages while retaining the target, ascends, or opens overview when nothing remains; Tab
//   leaves an input for the next control; Backspace edits narrowing; Space separates word prefixes;
//   `.` opens verbs; the unshifted hints physical key is `Slash` (`/` on US layouts, `?` accepted
//   shifted); `primary+K` opens the palette. Reserved punctuation never narrows
// - Precedence is engaged input, modal occurrence, targeted item, focused scene, app-wide command,
//   reducer key. Platform editing chords win while engaged. Modifier chords invoke directly;
//   bare single-letter `Key` values are accelerators only in the verb layer. Duplicate shortcuts
//   in one static Toolbar/Commands/entity/global scope are errors; platform editing chords warn
// - Press, focus, and hover enter the same semantic operations as keyboard routes. The remaining
//   pointer/touch adapters — right-click/long-press verbs, empty-region focus, and drag/drop — stay
//   in the Plan's decided-implementation ledger. Platform focus and reducer focus synchronize
// - `pressed`, `focused`, and `hovered` are bare postfix design conditions for a control;
//   `when FocusBar is active` is the named region-focus form. These existing words parse through
//   `ID`/layout-word seams and are spelling-validated, not reserved as grammar keywords. `focused`
//   means the target control in every modality; `active` means the currently focused region
// - An entity data block orders default verbs with `commands A, B` and withholds a command using
//   `commands hide C`. Any view may promote commands through its `Commands { ... }` supplied slot
//   and exclude inherited defaults with `hide C`. Verb order is view Commands, rendered inner
//   controls, entity defaults, then the remaining applicable catalog alphabetically. Repeated
//   visible labels fold onto the first verb in that priority order
// - `press key "..."`, `narrow "..."`, `expect target "..."`, `expect focus region "..."`, and
//   `expect verbs "...", "..."` extend tests through ID-headed, spelling-validated seams. They
//   dispatch reducer operations directly rather than synthetic DOM events. `beginTest` resets
//   attention. The existing `interaction` capture becomes `{ outline, attention }`; no duplicate
//   capture domain is registered
// - A command with open slots enters reducer-owned verb-pending state. The mounted chooser,
//   `(search)` store picker, scalar input, directional scroll fallback, and off-window loop-row
//   targeting stay in the Plan's decided-implementation ledger. Descending reaches an item's inner
//   controls; Escape ascends; re-aim and modal focus-stack behavior are implemented
//
// THE INTERACTION SYSTEM, PART FOUR: GENERATED SURFACES AND KEY ALLOCATION. This tranche renders
// the semantic outline and attention state as the affordances a person can actually see. It adds
// no language grammar: these layers are runtime policy, and `Hint` and `Overview` are ordinary
// element defaults in the mounted design (`Design - Keyboard driven apps.md`, KEY-D13; `Plan`, T5).
//
// This tranche decides:
// - The app host mounts one interaction-layer host above app content as a sibling after the toast
//   layer. Hints, overview, the verb menu, and the palette are pure renderings of outline snapshots,
//   attention snapshots, and current command bindings. They never enter navigation history, and a
//   hidden layer sets `accessibilityElementsHidden` so it is absent from accessibility traversal
// - `TR-interaction-layers.tsx` owns those renderings. Hints anchor to the last cached app-relative
//   bounds of controls and items and stretch across region bounds; overview lists the mounted
//   regions; the verb menu preserves the reducer's tiers and shows both active accelerators and
//   shadowed shortcuts; the palette lists every titled command and entity in the generated catalog
//   and narrows labels with the same locale-aware word-prefix subsequence matcher as attention.
//   A shared measurement registrar supplies app-relative bounds to the outline and every layer;
//   opening a surface never performs synchronous measurement or changes layout
// - `TR-interaction-allocation.ts` owns key allocation as pure runtime policy. It preserves an
//   identity's previous assignment while that identity remains present, considers new identities
//   in canonical identity order so render reordering cannot reshuffle keys, segments labels for the
//   active locale, and tries the first free label letter, then the next distinctive label-derived
//   letter, then deterministic two-letter sequences. Reducer keys and explicit command shortcuts
//   or accelerators are excluded, so generated affordances never shadow authored or system behavior
// - The first hardware-key dispatch records keyboard presence; before that, keyboard affordances
//   are not rendered. `press key` exercises the same dispatch seam in a test. The hints key toggles
//   hints, Escape with nothing left to clear opens overview, `.` opens the target's verb menu, and
//   `primary+K` opens the always-present palette
// - Generated surface copy is stable and visible: headings are `Interaction hints`, `Interaction
//   overview`, `Actions for <target>`, and `Command palette`; allocated rows expose an uppercase key
//   and label as `<KEY> — <label>`. These strings let product journeys prove that a layer exists
//   only after its key, rather than vacuously asserting text already supplied by app content
project {
   id "wordflower"
   name "WordFlower"
   version "1.0.0"
   DefaultApp WordFlower
   remote none
   license MIT
}

// The app is rooted in a view: the shell, which lays the navigator and the focus bar out as ordinary
// content. Tao mounts a navigator around a view root, so nothing here names one. Overlay and toast
// hosting are universal capabilities, so no app declares them.
app WordFlower {
   Name "WordFlower"
   Design WordFlowerDesign
   view WordFlowerShell(WordFlowerNavigator)
   Datasource DeviceStore // offline-first: the device owns the writing; sync is a variant
}

app WordFlowerDrawer = WordFlower with {
   Name "WordFlower - Drawer Preview"
   view WordFlowerShell(WordFlowerDrawerNavigator) // the same shell, a different navigator in it
}

// The explicit dark preview mounts a complete dark design and keeps the portable tab surface on
// every target, so the preview does not depend on the host operating system's current appearance.
app WordFlowerDark = WordFlower with {
   Name "WordFlower - Dark"
   Design WordFlowerDarkDesign
   view WordFlowerShell(WordFlowerTabsNavigator)
}

app WordFlowerInstantDB = WordFlower with {
   Name "WordFlower - InstantDB"
   Datasource WordFlowerInstantDBStore
}

app WordFlowerLocalInstantDB = WordFlower with {
   Name "WordFlower - Local InstantDB"
   Datasource WordFlowerLocalInstantDBStore
}

// The read net: a guard site that does not name an exceptional read case sends it here. The runtime
// supplies every case; WordFlower restyles loading and error and keeps the runtime's missing and
// unauthorized sentences.
guard default {
   loading -> Spinner()
   error -> Message { TextMultiline("Could not load this: { Message }") [body] }
}

fixture StudioWorkspace {
   Novel = create Workspace {
      Name: "Novel"
}  }

scenarios WorkspaceRow "states" {
   fixture StudioWorkspace
   device phone
   appearance light
   network online
   locale "en"
   scenario "novel" {
      render (Workspace: Novel)
      press #openWorkspace
      press label "Workspace title"
      enter "Novel Novel" into label "Workspace title"
      press #saveWorkspace
}  }

// This focused state forces every L1 scenario seam in a real WordFlower view. `Revert` is required
// by the view but intentionally omitted here, so Studio installs the invocation-recording stand-in.
// No fixture is needed because the entry has no handles or preparation.
scenarios SavedToast "interaction" {
   device phone
   scenario "held" {
      render ()
      press down #revertSave
      advance 600.ms
      press up #revertSave
      hover #revertSave
      focus #revertSave
}  }

scenarios WordFlower "devices" {
   fixture StudioWorkspace
   scenario "phone" {
      device phone
      appearance light
      network online
      locale "en"
   }
   scenario "tabletDark" {
      device tablet 1024 x 1366
      appearance dark
      network offline
      locale pseudolocale
      direction rightToLeft
}  }

scenarios WordFlowerDark "dark theme" {
   fixture StudioWorkspace
   device tablet 1024 x 1366
   appearance dark
   network offline
   locale "en"
   scenario "library" { }
}

// A configured datasource is a named value like any other. The bare constructor block is the
// canonical surface spelling for the same refinement operation that explicit `with` exposes;
// deriving from an existing datasource keeps `with` visible.
datasource DeviceStore = Local {
   StorageKey "WordFlowerData"
}

// The public sync demo uses the hosted app. InstantDB's default endpoints reach Instant Cloud.
datasource WordFlowerInstantDBStore = InstantDB {
   StorageKey "WordFlowerData"
   AppId "9faf89c0-c15c-49b4-bf3f-3b5b2cd9a19f"
}

// Local development keeps the same app ID in the repository's self-hosted fixture.
datasource WordFlowerLocalInstantDBStore = InstantDB {
   StorageKey "WordFlowerData"
   AppId "9faf89c0-c15c-49b4-bf3f-3b5b2cd9a19f"
   ApiURI "http://localhost:9020"
   WebsocketURI "ws://localhost:9020/runtime/session"
}
